      "math_academy_id": "e-14827",
      "problem": "Solve for $n$ where $n^2 +12n = 0.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we factor the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+12n & =0 \\\\ n(n+12) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the zero product rule to get \n\n$$\nn=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad n+12=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives \n\n$$\nn=0 \\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad n=-12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $n=-12,0.$",
